          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Ln6aAybugzTCDMamhLPQSh",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid search combining lexical and dense vector approaches.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - concept/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Two independent sources; one provides concrete hybrid retrieval pattern details.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g., BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets. The merged results are commonly ranked using reciprocal rank fusion, which captures both exact term matches and semantic similarity.\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either lexical or vector search alone, particularly on queries that mix rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs) with natural language intent. It has become the default retrieval pattern in production RAG systems.\\n\\n## Reciprocal Rank Fusion\\n\\nReciprocal rank fusion (RRF) is a merging strategy for combining ranked result sets from multiple retrieval methods. It provides a robust mechanism for aggregating results without requiring explicit score normalization across heterogeneous ranking systems.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: Hybrid retrieval overview and production patterns (eval://update-over-duplicate/sample-08)\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
